<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The design system gallery, in the debug source set only.

      A `BuildConfig.DEBUG` branch inside the main source set would have compiled the gallery into
      the release APK and relied on the shrinker to remove it. This cannot: the release variant
      never sees these sources, so there is nothing to strip and nothing to get wrong.

      It gets its own launcher entry, which means a second icon beside the app in debug builds.
      That is deliberate — the gallery has to be reachable without a navigation route, and M5 owns
      the app's real navigation.
    -->
    <application>
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.GalleryActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/gallery_launcher_label"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The five-tab shell, as if the app were configured. Reached by an instrumented test
          (HabitsShellNavigationTest), which can launch a non-exported activity because
          instrumentation runs against this app.

          `exported="false"` and no launcher entry, unlike the gallery beside it, because this one
          hosts the real SyncStatusScreen — and that screen can disconnect a live Notion connection.
          A debug build is still somebody's phone.

          ⚠ The cost is that `adb shell am start` CANNOT open it: the shell uid gets
          "Permission Denial: ... not exported from uid", and `run-as <pkg> am start` fails too
          ("package=com.android.shell does not belong to uid"). To look at the shell by hand, flip
          this to `true`, `installDebug`, screenshot, and flip it back — which is what M5.2 did.
        -->
        <!--
          The Week screen over SampleHabits, with no Room and no Notion behind it (M6.3).

          Exported, unlike ShellHarnessActivity below, and safe to be: it holds sample data in
          memory and touches no repository, so there is nothing here that could damage a live
          workspace. It exists because Week only shows habits once a connection has imported some,
          and connecting costs the USER a token paste — which would have made the app's most
          important screen one nobody could look at while building it.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.WeekPreviewActivity
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.WeekPreviewActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <!--
          The habit list against sample data (M7.5). Same reason and same safety as the Week preview
          above: the screen shows nothing until a Notion connection has imported habits, and it is
          in no Figma frame at all — so it has to be looked at before it is shown to anyone.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.ManageHabitsPreviewActivity
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.ManageHabitsPreviewActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <!--
            M7.8's mapping screen. It reads the live schema on open, so without a connection the real
            screen can only show its "could not read the schema" state — and it carries the note the
            user designed from prose, which has to be looked at.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.EditMappingPreviewActivity
            (add the extra `state` with value `dropping`, `added` or `offline`; a double dash cannot
            appear in an XML comment, so see the activity's KDoc for the whole command)
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.EditMappingPreviewActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <!--
          The Month view against sample data (M8.1). Same reason and same safety as the Week
          preview: the screen shows nothing until a Notion connection has imported habits, and its
          arrows are worth watching move rather than reasoning about — a month steps and the grid
          reshapes under it.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.MonthPreviewActivity
            (add the extra `state` with value `pending` or `failed` for the empty screen; a double
            dash cannot appear in an XML comment, so see the activity's KDoc for the whole command)
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.MonthPreviewActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <!--
          The Year view against sample data (M8.3). Same reason and same safety as the previews
          above. The thing worth looking at here is the horizontal scroll: the grid is wider than
          the card and scrolls itself to today on first composition, which is the opposite of what
          the web prototype does.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.YearPreviewActivity
            (add the extra `state` with value `pending` or `failed` for the empty screen; a double
            dash cannot appear in an XML comment, so see the activity's KDoc for the whole command)
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.YearPreviewActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <!--
          The Stats view against sample data (M9.1). Same reason and same safety as the previews
          above. At M9.1 there is deliberately almost nothing on it: the header, the three states
          and an empty content column. It exists now because each of the four sections that fill
          that column arrives in its own step, and this is what each of them is looked at through.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.StatsPreviewActivity
            (add the extra `state` with value `pending` or `failed` for the empty screen; a double
            dash cannot appear in an XML comment, so see the activity's KDoc for the whole command)
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.StatsPreviewActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <!--
          M9.8. Seeds SampleHabits into the REAL database and opens the shell on top of it, which is
          the only way to walk the check-off loop from Week to Stats without a Notion connection -
          and connecting costs the user a token paste.

          Exported, and safe to be, because the safety is in the activity rather than in this flag:
          it reads the stored mapping first and REFUSES when anything is connected. That guards the
          harm the shell's own exported="false" is aimed at, and guards it better - a check-off on a
          connected build would queue a push against the user's real workspace, which no manifest
          attribute prevents.

            adb shell am start -n com.cristianprincipi.habits/com.cristianprincipi.habits.gallery.SeedActivity
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.SeedActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />

        <activity
            android:name=".gallery.ShellHarnessActivity"
            android:exported="false"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.MyHabitApp" />
    </application>

</manifest>
